blkid
Usage: blkid [BLOCKDEV]...

Print UUIDs of all filesystems, or of the block devices named. Reports
the filesystem type and label too, by reading each device's superblock.

	blkid			every device it can see
	blkid /dev/sda1		just that one

On vos this is mostly a way to confirm what the machine actually exposes.
The stick you booted from appears; the host's internal disk does not,
because no NVMe or SATA driver is compiled in. A device that has no
driver cannot be read, mounted, or written -- which is the guarantee,
enforced by absence rather than by policy.

See also: findfs, mount, df, blockdev
